1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:03,880
Greetings to all subscribers and viewers of my channel.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:13,200
Today we will look at a few interesting points that people encounter during the tuning process.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:15,400
Question.

4
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:24,200
The first one goes like this: "I tuned a Tesla transformer to a quarter-wave based on your calculations."

5
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:27,780
"Now I want to retune it to a half-wave."

6
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:34,600
"But when I calculate the frequencies, the math doesn't add up."

7
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:43,700
"And if I switch to the frequency I calculated mathematically, the coil just won't tune."

8
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,040
"So what should I do about it?"

9
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:47,400
"And are the calculations even correct?"

10
00:00:48,400 --> 00:01:01,560
This schematic diagram I've shown here conceptually illustrates the standing wave distribution in a Tesla transformer.

11
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:06,460
The same applies to a grounded "grenade" coil system.

12
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:17,420
How currents and voltage are distributed when the system is set to a quarter-wave versus when it's set to a half-wave.

13
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:33,720
Essentially, to lock in the moment when our system is at a quarter-wave, we need to get maximum voltage at the upper, hot end of the Tesla transformer, on its top load.

14
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:40,540
Meanwhile, the current maximum will be at the lower end of the Tesla transformer.

15
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:46,000
This part here is the grounding wire and its wave distributions.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,920
The thing is, people usually only look at this part and forget about what's happening in the grounding at that moment.

17
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:02,070
But in the grounding, the pattern is in anti-phase to what's happening in the resonator.

18
00:02:03,190 --> 00:02:17,590
Therefore, if the entire system aligns to a quarter-wave, the whole system actually forms a complete picture, resulting in this half-wave distribution.

19
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:23,530
But only a quarter of the standing wave resides inside our Tesla transformer.

20
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:31,470
We get a different, more interesting situation when the system shifts to a half-wave.

21
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:42,570
In this case, the peak voltage point moves from the hot end of the Tesla transformer to the middle of the coil.

22
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:56,470
Meanwhile, the current distributes differently since there's a 90-degree phase shift between them, and the frequency here is higher so that this point shifts as the frequency increases.

23
00:02:57,030 --> 00:03:03,550
The wavelength shortens, and it distributes differently along the length of our coil.

24
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:07,590
That's why we get this kind of ratio.

25
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:16,750
With a half-wave distribution on our Tesla transformer, the current will be in anti-phase at its ends.

26
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:23,930
At the same time, as always, let's not forget about the grounding part we have here.

27
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:30,050
A complete shape is formed here too, except now it results in a full wave.

28
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:35,970
If you look at the system as a whole, only a half-wave falls on our resonator.

29
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:41,350
What calculations does a person typically make?

30
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:49,770
For example, I'll show the calculations for my actual coil to make it clear using its example.

31
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:53,630
The logic behind it is simple.

32
00:03:55,350 --> 00:04:12,290
A person calculates the Tesla transformer, takes the quarter-wave resonance frequency, multiplies it by 2, and gets the frequency for a half-wave on this resonator.

33
00:04:12,890 --> 00:04:22,130
The thing is, this is where idealized math kicks in, and for those who believe in it—all these formulas and such—it doesn't work.

34
00:04:22,790 --> 00:04:41,010
While you can still calculate the wave velocity via the known speed of light for a quarter-wave resonance up to a frequency of 1.5 MHz, you can no longer apply these calculations to a half-wave resonator.

35
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:44,090
The margins of error will be massive.

36
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:50,010
And now, in practice, we'll see what kind of errors occur in reality.

37
00:04:50,710 --> 00:05:04,510
To move on to the practical part, we first need to make a simple device to track the voltage antinode in our coil.

38
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:23,710
It's made from a 65 cm piece of copper wire folded almost in half, resulting in a 30 cm system from the neon bulb to the point where we hold it with our fingers.

39
00:05:25,490 --> 00:05:34,990
It's a copper wire system with a neon bulb soldered to the end, and the connection looks like this.

40
00:05:35,810 --> 00:05:42,170
We solder one contact to the copper and bend the other contact into this shape.

41
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:49,310
Now we have a convenient probe for locating the voltage antinode on the Tesla transformer.

42
00:05:50,250 --> 00:06:06,490
We will feed the frequency into the Tesla transformer using a laboratory signal generator, and by changing its frequency, we will check this indicator to see if the math...

43
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:16,770
...actually tells us the truth—that multiplying the quarter-wave resonance frequency gives us the half-wave—and look at the issues tied to this.

44
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:28,450
Before you is our test sample, my Tesla transformer, with a conductor length of 149 meters and a calculated frequency of 500 kHz.

45
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:40,290
This transformer is built on a white plastic ventilation duct frame, using a wire with a cross-section of 0.96 mm.

46
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:52,930
A top load was also chosen for it, matching the wave distribution along the wire length with the LC resonance using an additional top load.

47
00:06:53,370 --> 00:07:08,830
In this case, the Tesla transformer is currently connected to ground, and there is a single turn of conductor at the base of the Tesla transformer coil, which we will feed the signal to from the lab...

48
00:07:08,830 --> 00:07:10,190
...generator.

49
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:17,830
First, let's look at tuning the Tesla transformer to a quarter-wave.

50
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:24,630
Reminding you that at a quarter-wave, our voltage antinode will be located right at this point.

51
00:07:24,630 --> 00:07:34,830
And accordingly, the neon lamp should light up at this point, and as it moves toward the base of the transformer, it will go out.

52
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:40,110
I am applying a frequency of 500 kHz.

53
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:48,090
We can see the neon lamp is lit, and as I lower it, it goes out.

54
00:07:48,090 --> 00:07:55,240
In other words, the voltage antinode at the end of the high-voltage transformer winding is clearly visible.

55
00:07:55,730 --> 00:08:00,470
The neon lamp will also light up near the top load.

56
00:08:01,170 --> 00:08:08,300
This part usually doesn't cause much trouble, and most people manage it successfully.

57
00:08:09,170 --> 00:08:18,250
If they use the calculations I provided earlier on my channel, all these steps are covered, and you can calculate it using them.

58
00:08:19,010 --> 00:08:22,510
Let's also record the frequency in this experiment.

59
00:08:23,090 --> 00:08:27,030
There it is, visible on the lab signal generator at 500 kHz.

60
00:08:28,730 --> 00:08:30,210
The neon lamp is lit.

61
00:08:32,030 --> 00:08:35,810
Now let's switch our system to half-wave mode.

62
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:41,750
According to idealized math, we multiply our frequency by 2.

63
00:08:43,230 --> 00:08:48,610
Therefore, I set the frequency here to 1 MHz.

64
00:08:49,630 --> 00:08:58,030
Now, at this frequency, the neon lamp at the hot end of the Tesla transformer has gone out.

65
00:08:58,030 --> 00:09:08,730
And we can check if the voltage antinode has actually moved to the center of our coil.

66
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,740
Moving the indicator along.

67
00:09:22,710 --> 00:09:26,250
And as you can see, the lamp isn't lighting up anywhere.

68
00:09:27,790 --> 00:09:30,550
Maybe we have some minor deviations.

69
00:09:31,270 --> 00:09:33,350
Let's try to look for them.

70
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:38,910
Let's say I change the frequency to 1.1 MHz.

71
00:09:41,390 --> 00:09:43,150
There is no distribution whatsoever.

72
00:09:44,770 --> 00:09:48,250
So it's a clear indicator that our math isn't working.

73
00:09:48,370 --> 00:09:50,110
How do we find the half-wave?

74
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:56,510
We place our indicator near the middle of the Tesla transformer.

75
00:09:57,470 --> 00:09:59,430
Roughly in the center of the winding.

76
00:09:59,850 --> 00:10:01,810
And we start increasing the frequency.

77
00:10:03,350 --> 00:10:06,270
Sweeping smoothly by 10 kHz steps.

78
00:10:08,650 --> 00:10:12,670
And we see the exact moment the lamp lights up brightly.

79
00:10:14,710 --> 00:10:25,410
The point of maximum bulb brightness corresponds to the half-wave frequency for our Tesla transformer.

80
00:10:25,950 --> 00:10:33,150
Checking the indicator now, the neon lamp does not light up at the hot end.

81
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:35,590
The glow is in the center.

82
00:10:36,390 --> 00:10:40,290
And it also doesn't light up at the base of the Tesla transformer.

83
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:45,590
Now let's see what we actually got in reality.

84
00:10:45,990 --> 00:10:46,970
What is the frequency?

85
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:53,130
Let's shift our attention to the lab signal generator.

86
00:10:54,590 --> 00:11:02,250
And we can see that the half-wave resonance frequency corresponds to 1480 kHz.

87
00:11:03,350 --> 00:11:08,310
So we can conclude: wow, what a massive difference.

88
00:11:08,890 --> 00:11:11,670
We clearly see that the math doesn't work here.

89
00:11:13,510 --> 00:11:21,910
We'll also now measure the spectral lines of this Tesla transformer using a spectrum analyzer.

90
00:11:22,390 --> 00:11:29,850
Maybe I did something wrong somewhere, but the spectrum analyzer will definitely show us the entire spectral series.

91
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:32,690
Moving on to the experiment with the spectrum analyzer.

92
00:11:33,350 --> 00:11:45,330
The optimal option for tracking the resonant transformer's frequency is to pick it up via an antenna, without connecting any probes directly to the system.

93
00:11:45,990 --> 00:11:52,170
Please note that the lab signal generator is currently disconnected from the single turn.

94
00:11:52,970 --> 00:11:56,230
In fact, it's completely powered off so as not to cause interference.

95
00:11:56,230 --> 00:12:03,610
With this experiment, we are testing the accuracy of our Tesla transformer's quarter-wave tuning.

96
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:13,790
You can notice that a spectral peak occasionally appears on the device.

97
00:12:14,390 --> 00:12:17,510
This little spike jumping up right here.

98
00:12:18,010 --> 00:12:19,190
It's very clearly visible.

99
00:12:19,710 --> 00:12:23,050
I've placed a marker on it.

100
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:29,650
The marker frequency shown at its peak is 500.1 kHz.

101
00:12:30,750 --> 00:12:34,570
What does the appearance of this spike indicate?

102
00:12:34,890 --> 00:12:48,670
It shows that this Tesla transformer reacts when exposed to external radio waves from distant radio transmitters or, as you might have seen, a passing spike.

103
00:12:48,790 --> 00:12:51,170
For instance, it could be lightning activity somewhere.

104
00:12:52,110 --> 00:12:54,370
By picking it up, it responds.

105
00:12:54,670 --> 00:12:57,730
And now, let's feed in our 500 kHz frequency.

106
00:12:59,110 --> 00:13:04,750
We see that the spectral peak shoots up immediately by several orders of magnitude.

107
00:13:05,730 --> 00:13:12,970
And when I bring my hand close, the amplitude drops.

108
00:13:13,230 --> 00:13:15,050
This indicates precise tuning.

109
00:13:15,910 --> 00:13:23,030
Also, at this frequency, the subsequent spectral peaks of our transformer's harmonic series start to show up.

110
00:13:23,710 --> 00:13:30,050
Now I will change the frequency, and we will observe the spectrogram further.

111
00:13:30,990 --> 00:13:32,830
I'm starting to increase the frequency.

112
00:13:34,750 --> 00:13:37,210
The step size will be 10 kHz.

113
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:43,460
We can see the detuning.

114
00:13:45,060 --> 00:13:46,800
The amplitude is dropping.

115
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:50,960
And the peak is shifting.

116
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:56,480
Right now, we are at our mathematically calculated frequency of one megahertz.

117
00:13:56,660 --> 00:13:59,760
See how small it is on this transformer.

118
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:01,340
Raising it further.

119
00:14:02,460 --> 00:14:03,640
We observe a rise.

120
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:08,700
Let's see where the peak will be for this frequency range.

121
00:14:13,310 --> 00:14:15,350
Our peak...

122
00:14:20,170 --> 00:14:25,730
...corresponds to 1.5 MHz.

123
00:14:25,930 --> 00:14:32,450
Our measurements using the neon lamp were remarkably accurate.

124
00:14:32,450 --> 00:14:37,790
During those tests, a frequency of 1480 kHz was found.

125
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:43,590
Now I'll move the marker so we can check the frequency.

126
00:14:48,830 --> 00:14:50,170
Here is the frequency.

127
00:14:51,270 --> 00:14:54,050
We see that the marker has moved to the top.

128
00:14:54,130 --> 00:14:59,130
And there it is, matching 1500 kHz.

129
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:12,810
Meanwhile, if we go back to our Tesla transformer, at this frequency, as you remember, the neon lamp glows in the center.

130
00:15:14,670 --> 00:15:34,930
Now I'll bump up the voltage slightly to get it to light up because, for a half-wave distribution, I just added literally 4 Volts, and that was already enough for the neon bulb...

131
00:15:34,930 --> 00:15:35,750
...to light up.

132
00:15:36,710 --> 00:15:49,170
Because a quarter-wave signal is higher in terms of voltage antinode peak than a half-wave distribution.

133
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:59,000
What interesting things can we notice on the spectrum analyzer?

134
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:07,700
We can see that the following frequencies appear.

135
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:10,340
There they are, the spectral peaks showing up.

136
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:16,980
And this peak bouncing up here is our 500 kHz frequency.

137
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:32,100
In other words, looking at the frequency range of our Tesla transformer, when we set it to a half-wave, the 500 kHz frequency and subsequent frequencies are also excited.

138
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:38,560
Now I will sweep through the frequencies, and we'll record its frequency response.

139
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:41,620
Let's move on.

140
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,060
So, I will keep stepping by 10 kHz.

141
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:54,290
Moving to the next maximum.

142
00:16:55,330 --> 00:16:59,410
Note the grid squares where the next peak was.

143
00:17:00,370 --> 00:17:03,450
And at the next peak, we'll see an increase in amplitude.

144
00:17:03,910 --> 00:17:05,490
There goes the amplitude, starting to rise.

145
00:17:06,870 --> 00:17:08,330
Here is its peak.

146
00:17:10,790 --> 00:17:19,410
The frequency on the lab signal generator is 2990 kHz.

147
00:17:22,650 --> 00:17:24,850
Let's check the frequency.

148
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:31,740
The peak has shifted.

149
00:17:33,420 --> 00:17:37,740
And we see the frequency is 2990 kHz.

150
00:17:38,060 --> 00:17:39,820
Continuing up the frequency range.

151
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:52,100
And here we see the peak growing.

152
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:54,000
The next frequency is...

153
00:17:59,020 --> 00:18:04,640
...3840 kHz.

154
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:13,510
Let's see if it matches.

155
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:18,480
Everything matches.

156
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:19,940
3840.

157
00:18:21,060 --> 00:18:26,480
Now we fully know the frequency series of this Tesla transformer.

158
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:28,960
Within the frequencies we can actually work with.

159
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:38,940
The whole scale spans from a starting 100 kHz up to a 5 MHz band where we capture the spectral series.

160
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:50,400
All the experiments I did with the spectrum analyzer can also be done using an oscilloscope and a laboratory signal generator.

161
00:18:50,620 --> 00:18:52,940
It's just that the workflow of the experiment will be different.

162
00:18:53,840 --> 00:19:01,400
We move this exact same antenna from the spectrum analyzer over to the oscilloscope.

163
00:19:01,900 --> 00:19:06,240
Or we hang its probe in the air near the Tesla transformer.

164
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:13,580
And then we vary the frequency on the single turn using the lab signal generator.

165
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:25,640
And we log all the peaks and amplitudes recorded by the oscilloscope as the frequency changes on the lab signal generator.

166
00:19:26,540 --> 00:19:28,620
The frequency picture will be exactly the same.

167
00:19:28,780 --> 00:19:34,980
It's just that having a spectrum analyzer makes this process far more efficient and faster.

168
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:36,840
That's the only difference.

169
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:42,120
Now we can actually observe the frequency distributions.

170
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:47,200
Quarter, half-wave, three-quarters, and four-quarters (which is a full wave).

171
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:52,340
These are the frequencies we can still realistically work with using electronics.

172
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:56,960
We can see that they do not conform to basic math.

173
00:19:58,160 --> 00:20:04,840
For instance, if we determined the full wavelength mathematically from the quarter-wave, we would multiply 500 kHz by 4.

174
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,900
It should correspond to 2 MHz.

175
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:09,260
But look at what frequency we get in reality.

176
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:14,360
Therefore, I don't recommend relying strictly on math.

177
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:21,160
So in this case, we cross off that question about how the person calculated it.

178
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:24,800
Now, the core of the physical phenomenon.

179
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:31,120
Why does this interesting anomaly happen with the frequency distribution?

180
00:20:32,780 --> 00:20:39,760
The fact is, these effects manifest very prominently in resonant systems.

181
00:20:40,260 --> 00:20:49,300
In classic systems where wave distribution occurs, they don't behave quite like they do in wave resonators.

182
00:20:49,940 --> 00:20:52,620
Velocity factors are applied there too.

183
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:57,620
But in our case, it shows up very distinctly.

184
00:20:58,620 --> 00:21:03,320
Let's look at the first case, where the system is set to a quarter-wave.

185
00:21:03,440 --> 00:21:06,360
The combined system with grounding forms a half-wave.

186
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:14,080
Here, the helical wave resonator actually has half the impact compared to when the system is set to a half-wave.

187
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:20,980
Meaning, in that second case, a half-wave fits inside the coil's helix itself, whereas here it's only a quarter.

188
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:23,100
Thus, its influence increases in this scenario.

189
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:35,060
And an interesting effect is observed: the wave velocity in a helical wave resonator actually increases compared to the velocity of a wave traveling simply through a straight conductor.

190
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:38,280
Paradoxical as it sounds, that's where the intrigue lies.

191
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:49,980
It's also worth noting that the wave distributes not just along the grounding wire, but also within the soil where this ground is connected.

192
00:21:50,980 --> 00:21:59,760
Meaning, from the cold end to the end of the wire, the wave effectively propagates not only through this hardware section but through the ground as well.

193
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:01,340
It all depends on the soil conditions.

192
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:11,240
And so it turns out, as we know, that with an increase in frequency, the physical wavelength in meters shortens.

193
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:23,900
Because of this, in order to pack exactly a half-wave into our wire length of the helical wave resonator, we have to raise the frequency.

196
00:22:24,080 --> 00:22:25,800
That's when it will fit.

197
00:22:25,940 --> 00:22:29,880
We reduce the physical meters and fit it into the length of the coil.

198
00:22:31,360 --> 00:22:36,620
Now, a reasonable question arises, or will arise, for the viewers.

199
00:22:36,620 --> 00:22:42,540
"Why all this dancing around just to switch the coil to a half-wave?"

200
00:22:43,080 --> 00:22:49,500
The fact is, to build a functional system, a half-wave oscillator is absolutely mandatory.

201
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:55,060
Without it, you won't get any serious energy output.

202
00:22:55,180 --> 00:23:03,360
Therefore, there's no escaping this half-wave tuning of the receiving coil.

203
00:23:03,360 --> 00:23:08,320
And once again, I'll draw your attention to the design of this coil.

204
00:23:09,340 --> 00:23:17,720
It is wound with thick copper wire specifically to eliminate active resistance from it.

205
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,980
I will be building my own generator down the road.

206
00:23:24,280 --> 00:23:28,080
On that note, good luck with your experiments, and all the best.

207
00:23:28,080 --> 00:23:38,700
In the next video, which I'll try to shoot as soon as possible, we will address another interesting question that was asked.

208
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:45,180
How does one Tesla transformer affect another through its fields?

209
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:47,660
What kind of distributions occur?

210
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:57,000
How do you create modulation without using a laboratory signal generator and complex electronics?

211
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:58,260
It's going to be interesting!
